    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting way to frame it. Could you go back to the part about the pH levels and the reaction speed? I'm specifically looking for the chemical mechanism—like, how does an increase in alkalinity specifically affect the nucleophilic attack of the amino group on the carbonyl group? I need the technical details for my paper.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, let's delve deeper into the chemical mechanism. When the pH of the environment increases, becoming more alkaline, it affects the nucleophilicity of the amino group on the amino acid. In a more alkaline environment, the amino group tends to be deprotonated, forming an amino anion. This anion is a stronger nucleophile compared to its protonated form, which means it is more likely to attack the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the reducing sugar.\n\nHowever, this increased nucleophilicity comes with a caveat. As the pH rises, the carbonyl group of the reducing sugar also becomes more susceptible to nucleophilic attack, but the overall reaction rate can be hindered by the formation of a less reactive Schiff base intermediate. This intermediate can undergo various side reactions, such as Amadori rearrangement, which can lead to a complex mixture of products and potentially slow down the desired Maillard reaction pathway.\n\nMoreover, at higher pH levels, the concentration of hydroxide ions increases, which can lead to the hydrolysis of the Schiff base, reverting it back to the starting materials. This hydrolysis competes with the desired Maillard reaction, effectively reducing the rate of non-enzymatic browning",
      "source": "target",
